  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a qualitative analysis of four specific projects across only two Scandinavian cities, which may limit their applicability to different political and governance contexts.",
      "what": "This study conceptualizes the term 'smartwashing' to describe how urban initiatives selectively use narratives of smartness to gain legitimacy and political support. The researchers analyzed four smart mobility projects in Stavanger, Norway, and Gothenburg, Sweden, demonstrating how these projects align themselves with existing policy goals and local governance frameworks even when their actual impacts are incomplete or uncertain.",
      "why": "It provides a new analytical framework for understanding how smart city projects secure funding and political viability through performative framing rather than proven performance, revealing how 'smartness' functions as a powerful tool of contemporary urban governance."
    }
